WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.230
Speaker 1: There is a campaign to be Donald
Trump's vice president going on right now,

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.900
and there is really one person who is in that
campaign.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:13.009
And it is radical, repugnant.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:16.400
Republican Congresswoman Marjorie Taylor GREENE.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:21.029
Marjorie Taylor GREENE is openly running to
be Trump's VP.

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:24.670
She is trying to dissuade people like Ron
DeSantis.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:28.480
And when it looked like maybe Carrie Lake
would be the next governor of Arizona, Carrie

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.600
Lake from running partially because she wants
to be Trump's VP.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.140
She believes her best shot at being the vice
presidential pick is if Donald Trump is the

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.140
nominee.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.810
And we're going to get to that in a moment.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:43.590
Marjorie Taylor GREENE yesterday was confronted
with a very good question, a very obvious

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:44.710
question.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:49.739
If Trump is so strong, if Trump is so powerful,
if Trump is so revered in the Republican Party,

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:56.120
why did so many of the people that Donald
Trump endorsed lose and Marjorie Taylor GREENE

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:00.960
Just sort of like with a throw everything
at the wall and see if any of it makes sense.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.770
And so listen to how she answered the question.

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:05.750
Speaker 6: Trump endorsed candidates last
year.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.460
Speaker 7: Including one.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.470
I would say there's I would say it depends
on the district.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:14.750
I think there's a lot of varying factors.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:20.750
Redistricting happened and that was based
on the the census that was pretty much pre-COVID.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.460
A lot of people moved out of those districts
over the past few years.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:25.460
Speaker 1: Okay.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:26.460
So so.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.370
Oh, my goodness.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:34.479
So first of all, redistricting only matters
in congressional races.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:41.840
Trump's biggest losses in the contested races
of consequence, that would have made a difference.

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.939
Carrie Lake in Arizona, that's the entire
state.

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:46.790
Redistricting doesn't matter.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:52.229
Blake Masters in Arizona Senate races or the
entire state redistricting doesn't matter.

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:58.060
Doug Mastriano and Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania,
Tudor Dickson in Michigan.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.119
These are all statewide races.

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.680
Redistricting doesn't matter.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.840
Then Marjorie Taylor GREENE polls well, a
lot of people move during the pandemic to

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:12.030
other states unless you can make the case
that it was disproportionately Republicans

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.440
that moved.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:19.420
That also isn't really a good explanation
for why Trump's endorsements ended up falling

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.459
flat.

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:22.770
None of these explanations make sense.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:29.129
But remember, Marjorie Taylor GREENE is desperate
to be Trump's VP, so she has to come up with

00:02:29.129 --> 00:02:32.110
something other than, you know, a lot of people
kind of don't like Trump.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.640
Speaker 7: I think that plays a part.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.190
I think it's a candidate by candidate.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:41.269
Sometimes one campaign is run really well
and sometimes one is not.

00:02:41.269 --> 00:02:43.610
But I would also say the media plays a big
part.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.190
Speaker 1: So then we get to really just blame
the media.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:49.350
Speaker 7: Media leans pretty hard left.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:53.950
And so when Republican candidates, when we
run for office, not only are we running against

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:58.190
a Democrat opponent, we're also running against
the media a good bit of the time, and that's

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.190
really not fair.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.390
So I think there's a lot of factors at play.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.000
And President Trump's win record is is historic.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.690
Most of his candidates won.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:10.220
Very few of his candidates lost.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.260
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:16.080
And again, remember, even Republicans are
admitting to the absurdity of that statement,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.349
Trump's record, which he says, oh, we were
like 215 or something, you know, it's every

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:23.599
day it's a different number that he claims.

00:03:23.599 --> 00:03:34.489
You mostly endorse incumbents who usually
win or Republicans in safe red races.

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:36.549
That is not impressive.

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:41.629
If I was in the endorsement game, I could
have a really good endorsement record because

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:47.280
there are quite literally hundreds of races
in which it's obvious who's going to win.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.129
Would it really be meaningful?

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:53.099
Would it really be meaningful if I actually
called it in that way?

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:59.459
Because I hate to say it, a couple chimpanzees
could probably call most of the races where

00:03:59.459 --> 00:04:04.090
it mattered, where it was contested, where
it could have made a difference in the balance.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:05.569
Trump failed miserably.

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:07.110
Someone who also failed miserably in.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.490
They endorsements is Tulsi Gabbard.

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.069
